The excerpts describe how a baby can be covered under statutory health insurance through Familienversicherung (free family cover for dependants). They do not say whether your baby will be covered. Only your statutory health insurance fund (Krankenkasse) decides that.

Who is covered. Federal law says the children of members of a statutory health insurance fund are insured through Familienversicherung if the child:
- lives or usually stays in Germany;
- is not insured in their own right, compulsorily or voluntarily;
- is not exempt from insurance;
- is not mainly self-employed;
- has no regular total income above one seventh of the monthly reference amount ("ein Siebtel der monatlichen Bezugsgröße").
Children are covered until their 18th birthday, and longer under further conditions.

The main exception. A child is not covered this way if all three of these apply to the parent who is related to the child and is the member's spouse or civil partner:
- that parent is not a member of a statutory health insurance fund;
- that parent's total income regularly exceeds one twelfth of the annual earnings ceiling per month ("ein Zwölftel der Jahresarbeitsentgeltgrenze");
- that income is regularly higher than the member's.

The birth certificate. You need the baby's birth certificate to register the baby with the health insurance fund. You must register the birth within 1 week at the registry office responsible for the place of birth, and that office issues the certificate. If the baby is born in a hospital, birth centre or maternity clinic, the facility notifies the registry office.

What the excerpts do not cover. They do not say how to register the baby with the fund, which documents beyond the birth certificate it needs, or how a baby is insured if family cover does not apply. Ask your statutory health insurance fund. It decides whether your baby is covered. Private health insurance is outside what this service covers. A private insurer or an independent adviser handles those questions.
